  "updated": "2026-09-05T01:00:19.466649+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heat again an issue in the Piedmont today. Less\nso Saturday.\n\nTemperatures already in the upper 90s to near 100 in the\nPiedmont as of 1pm, and with dewpoints in/near 70, heat indices\nalready around 105. A heat advisory was issued for areas\nalong/east of a Lynchburg/Danville/Reidsville line this\nafternoon.\n\nMore clouds and potential better coverage of storms Saturday\nshould limit the heat a few degrees, but more-so Sunday when the\nfront is south of us.\n\nTemperatures head to near normal later Sunday into early next\nweek, but mid to late week the ridge expands northeast again to\nnudge temperatures above normal.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong to severe thunderstorms possible this\nafternoon/evening and again Saturday.\n\nA couple of organized thunderstorm clusters across the \nsouthern Great Lakes and mid/upper Ohio Valley this afternoon\ninto tonight, are expected to flow southeast into our region.\nHigh-res/cam models are all over the place with respect to where\nconvection moves in and/or develops. The HiResARW had a better\nhandle on what was happening this morning upstream. Since looked\nreasonable this afternoon. blended this in with the official\nforecast. Getting isolated storms in WV but still overall looks\nlike a scattered nature to the storms, potentially more\norganized from eastern WV north of I-64, into the Piedmont of\nVA, with secondary development occurring southside VA into the\nNC Piedmont by later this afternoon.\n\nThe threat for damaging wind gusts will extend southeastward into\nthe Mid-Atlantic this afternoon and evening amid a very warm and\nmoist airmass. Vertical shear will be weaker with southward extent,\nbut ample moisture (i.e. PW values from 1.8\" to 2\"), strong\nbuoyancy, and steep low-level lapse rates will support the potential\nfor strong outflow capable of wind damage. Bowing line\nsegments will be the primary storm mode here as well.\n\nOverall, flooding threat looks minimal given flow, but if there\nis training of storms, maybe some localized flooding.\n\nSaturday, strong to severe thunderstorms capable of damaging \ngusts are possible across portions of the central Appalachians \nand Carolinas.\n\nA mid-level trough across the western Atlantic retrogrades \nslightly on Saturday with an associated surface front expected \nto move slowly southward from the Mid-Atlantic to the Carolinas.\nSouth of this cold front, a very moist airmass will be in place\nwith strong instability, particularly in the NC Piedmont.  \nFarther west, downslope flow off the mountains may result in a \nmore stable environment in the western half of the Carolinas. \nThis signal varies within the guidance, and confidence is not \nhigh enough to lower probabilities across this area, but the \ngreater threat should remain farther east where mid to upper 70s\ndewpoints and around 30 knots of shear amid a front and height \nfalls aloft should support scattered strong to severe storms \ncapable of mostly damaging winds.\n\nTo the west and northwest, strong heating across eastern \nKentucky and vicinity should provide an unstable and uncapped \nairmass by Saturday afternoon. Mid-level flow will be relatively\nweak across this region (~20 knots) and therefore, effective \nshear and storm organization is expected to be weak. However, \nstrong heating and steep lapse rates will support some damaging \nwind threat Saturday afternoon/evening. Some guidance shows \nthese storms clustering and traversing the Appalachians and \ncontinuing into western Virginia and western North Carolina. The\nrelatively weak shear, and potential for lesser instability due\nto downslope flow across the eastern Appalachians casts \nsignificant doubt on this potential. \n\nModels are showing the front south of us Sunday but another\ncouple of impulses and some low level easterly flow allows for\nat least threat of more showers/isolated storms Sunday but\nleaning toward lower pops than NBM. Seems the southern Blue\nRidge into the Alleghanys of VA have a better chance than the\nrest of the area of seeing measurable rainfall.\n\nNext week so far seems rather dry.",
